    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "#<font color = #D683C5> **ASK (Hypothesis / Analysis Goal)**\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "8qWkHIs1f_TW"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The film industry releases hundreds of movies each year across various time periods. These different time periods present very different competitive conditions. Hence, an interesting question is if and how release timing affects box office performance. Such descriptive analysis may allow film distributors to examine how strategic timing decisions influence commercial success and thus improve release strategies to maximise box office sales.\n",
        "\n",
        "We will explore the correlation between different competitive release environments and box office outcomes while controlling for film quality using TMDb ratings as a benchmark.\n",
        "\n",
        "Competitive release environments are defined along the following dimensions:\n",
        "- Competition density: volume of films released simultaneously (crowded vs. clear windows)\n",
        "\n",
        "- Competitor scale: presence of major studio blockbusters versus smaller releases\n",
        "\n",
        "- Seasonal context: impact of releasing during peak periods (summer, holidays) versus off-peak months\n",
        "\n",
        "For the analysis of competitor scale, we define \"major studio films\" to be those released by any of the \"Big Six\" studios (Disney, Paramount, Universal, Warner Bros., Sony, 20th Century Fox). A film is \"released alongside\" a major studio film if the films' release dates have a difference of ≤ 7 days.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<font color = #FF0000> We note that the big six Major studios also have **key subsidiary companies** which we also have to account for.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Qa9PFgLbn1tC"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "<hr>"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "eag5HBGONZdn"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "#<font color = #806FDE> **PREPARE (Data Source Identification & Exploration)**\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "kttN60pJ64n4"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### **Requirements Analysis**\n",
        "\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "IuFZNxcdoUmJ"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "To tackle the problem, we need data about movies that includes the movie’s **title** and **release date**, the associated **production company**, its **average TMDb rating** (to indicate quality) and its **worldwide box office revenue** (as the outcome). This information must be alignable across sources using a consistent identifier (e.g. title + release year) so that ratings and revenue can be joined accurately.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Since this analysis focuses on the effect of release timing alongside major studio films, we restrict our dataset to film releases from **2000-2017**. This ensures our definition of \"major film studios\" as the “Big Six” remains consistent throughout the entire analysis period (due to acquisition of 20th Century Fox by Disney in December 2017). This coverage also ensures a large enough sample size sufficient for isolating the specific impact of release timing from broader market fluctuations, allowing us to compare “crowded” vs “non-crowded” release windows fairly. One summary entry per movie is sufficient (no need for daily ticket sales) as the target is total worldwide revenue.\n",
        "\n",
        "The key timing feature can be computed from the release date within a **±7 day window**. This window is used as it aligns with the film industry’s weekly release cycle, captures the critical opening-week competition where most revenue is generated and isolates direct competitive effects without introducing noise from temporally distant releases.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KuXdKtIt0j0m"
      }
    },
