    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "We note that during data preparation, using production company information to identify \"major studio films\" will require careful cleaning due to missing values and inconsistent representation of production companies. We observe the following issues:\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "**1) High number of rows with missing production companies:**\n",
        "In the full dataset, production_companies is missing for 11,335 out of 44,691 movies. After restricting release year to 2000 to 2017, production_companies remains missing for 7,177 out of 23,578 movies. This reduces the number of movies for comparison.\n",
        "\n",
        "**2) Multiple companies stored in one cell:**\n",
        "production_companies is stored as a single string with multiple companies separated by a pipe delimiter |. Hence, we must split and explode this column before we can count companies or create studio indicators.\n",
        "\n",
        "**3) Inconsistent naming issues:**\n",
        "Company names may appear in different forms (e.g. abbreviations, punctuation, parent company vs label names), which can fragment counts and affect the identification of major studios unless standardised.\n",
        "\n",
        "**4) Co-production:**\n",
        "Many movies list multiple production companies, so we need a consistent rule for classification (e.g. a film is tagged as \"major studio\" if any listed company is part of the \"big six\").\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "These issues may affect our analysis because missing or inconsistent production company information can lead to misclassification, under detection of competitive releases and selection bias if we drop missing rows. To address this, we will standardise production company names and treat missing production companies explicitly."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Mgt9MT1cRuDl"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "split_companies = df['production_companies'].dropna().str.split('|')\n",
