    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "**Quality Consideration**"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c1YtlSTOyE_u"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Overall, we observe that while the dataset covers a wide array of production entities, the frequency of film releases per company varies significantly. This disparity may introduce sampling bias during our analysis if the results are dominated by the output patterns of a few industry giants. In the plot showing the top 30 production companies, we observe a clear concentration among the \"Big Six\" studios (highlighted in red), such as Warner Bros. and Universal Pictures, which release more flims than the smaller companies like Toho Company or StudioCanal.\n",
        "\n",
        "Because these major studios command such a high volume of the theatrical calendar, the \"typical\" release window is often crowded. This suggests that different competitive pressures are present in the data but to varying degrees. To ensure a balanced analysis of how release timing affects box office revenue (our measure of popularity), we **must use TMDB Ratings** as a quality control filter. This allows us to determine whether a film's success is due to its inherent quality or simply because of the lack of competition from a \"Big Six\" blockbuster during its opening window. As part of our data preparation, we will also categorize films by studio tier to avoid the bias inherent in this high volume production skew."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4z9UTK-qMxJc"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "**Conclusion for Dataset 1: Worldwide Movie Dataset**"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "qWTCGpGtyIRP"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Despite Canal+ being the dominant production company, it is important to recognise that it produces many non-theatrical content alongside movies. While the dataset still contains some statistical noise, it remains sufficient to illustrate the concentration of market power among the \"Big Six\" studios, hence making it useful for our analysis.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "PHsxRIB3yNEA"
      }
    },
